WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.084
SOC 2 reports are commonly discussed as Type I or Type II.

00:00:04.084 --> 00:00:07.404
The difference is about timing and what the auditors evaluate.

00:00:07.404 --> 00:00:12.716
A Type I report evaluates whether controls are suitably designed at a specific point in time.

00:00:12.716 --> 00:00:16.700
It answers whether the control design appears appropriate for the selected criteria

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.692
and scope on a defined date.

00:00:18.692 --> 00:00:22.676
A Type II report evaluates whether controls are suitably designed and operated

00:00:22.676 --> 00:00:24.668
effectively over a defined review period.

00:00:24.668 --> 00:00:28.984
That period is often several months, depending on the engagement and customer expectations.

00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:32.968
Type I can be useful when an organization is starting its SOC

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:36.620
2 journey or wants an initial independent view of control design.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.936
It can show that the organization has put a control framework in place.

00:00:40.936 --> 00:00:46.248
Type II usually provides stronger operational assurance because it examines whether controls were performed over time.

00:00:46.248 --> 00:00:51.560
Customers often prefer Type II reports because they show sustained operation rather than only point-in-time design.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.876
Neither report type should be described as a badge that ends the work.

00:00:55.876 --> 00:00:58.200
Controls still need ownership, evidence, monitoring, maintenance,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.524
and improvement after the report is issued.

00:01:00.524 --> 00:01:02.516
Understanding the difference helps teams prepare.

00:01:02.516 --> 00:01:04.840
Type I emphasizes readiness on a date,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.160
while Type II emphasizes consistent operation during the audit period.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.812
For teams doing the work, Type II usually requires stronger habits.

00:01:11.812 --> 00:01:15.132
Controls need to happen repeatedly, exceptions need to be tracked,

00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:19.780
and evidence needs to show that the process was followed throughout the review window.
